Gateway inventory cache access (2026-09-25)
=========================================

The firmware, installed-package and installed-plugin read methods previously
returned warm cache entries before checking the gateway's current organization,
deletion state and the caller's site grants. The endpoints required their read
permission, but a permitted caller who knew another gateway UUID could receive
its cached inventory without passing the normal resource access check.

Each method now performs the existing gateway access check before its cache
lookup. The cache key also contains the organization UUID, so transferring a
gateway between tenants does not reuse the previous tenant's cached inventory.
An authorized cache hit retains the original payload and avoids device I/O.
A missing, deleted, foreign or inaccessible gateway returns the existing opaque
404 response, without constructing an adapter or returning cached inventory.
Endpoint authentication and permission checks remain unchanged.

Thirty-six new PostgreSQL/HTTP cases reproduce 24 old-code failures and retain
12 controls. They cover foreign callers, changed grants, unscoped or reassigned
gateways, deletion, both sides of an organization transfer, authorized hits,
cold denials and endpoint authentication/permission refusal. Separate incoming
TCP HTTP checks exercise these paths through the actual router and service.
Fixture identities and device inventory replies are synthetic; the database,
HTTP transport and cache implementation are real. Source-bound receipts record
the fresh full and focused results; these probes do not establish device login,
vendor TLS, hardware compatibility or enterprise acceptance.

This correction applies to these three cache lookups. It preserves the existing
five-minute inventory TTL and the process-local cache implementation. It does
not add distributed invalidation, configuration-version cache keys, or a general
transactional fence for user/session revocation during device I/O. Broader
active-parent and authority-revalidation work remains separate. No gateway,
organization or site row is modified by a cache read.
